{
  "abstract": "Let $B$ be the set of $N$ distinct consecutive positive odd Collatz states. For every finite nonempty set $C$ of $M$ positive integers, we prove $|BC|\\ge c_\\varepsilon(NM)^{2/3-\\varepsilon}$, uniformly over the source, all division exponents, and the magnitudes and prime factors of the multipliers. In particular, $|BB|\\ge c_\\varepsilon N^{4/3-\\varepsilon}$ improves the previous unrestricted exponent $49/38$. For each fixed $k\\ge2$, a common-threshold argument gives $|B^{(k)}|\\ge c_{k,\\varepsilon}N^{2^k/(2^{k-1}+1)-\\varepsilon}$, including exponents $8/5$ and $16/9$ at orders three and four. The losses are explicitly subpolynomial, with effective constants. The proof advances one factor along a retained Collatz edge: $xz\\to U(x)z$. Edge endpoints recover both factors, making the product graph simple at every order. A classical logarithmic-form bound excludes directed cycles above a polynomial height, and chronology controls degree imbalance. Counting edges in a topological order gives one lower bound; divisor moments and Fourier Hölder interpolation give the complementary growth from small states against all multipliers. Exact finite audits check the graph, boundary and divisor interfaces. The results impose stronger necessary multiplicative restrictions on arbitrary distinct orbit segments, but supply no incompatible upper bound and do not resolve Collatz convergence.",
